<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duarte Barbosa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1480–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duarte Barbosa was a Portuguese writer and official who served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a scribe and interpreter from around 1500. His Book of Duarte Barbosa, completed about 1516, is one of the earliest European accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> world, describing the towns of the East African coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, India and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. He was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1521 during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>'s voyage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa.jpg" alt="Duarte Barbosa" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Edward John Stanley, Baron Stanley, 1827-1903 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Voyage_Round_The_World_Signatures_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His father, Diogo Barbosa, was a servant of Álvaro of Braganza and sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1501 with the 3rd Portuguese India Armada under João da Nova. While his father was away, Duarte stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> with his uncle Gonçalo Gil Barbosa, a factor who had travelled with the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> in 1500. In 1502 Gonçalo was moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>, and Duarte went with him and learned Malayalam, the local language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1503 Barbosa interpreted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> in his dealings with the Rajah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannur">Cannanore</a>. In 1513 he signed a letter to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> as Clerk of Cannanore, and in 1514 Albuquerque used him as interpreter in an attempt to convert the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>. In 1515 he was sent to Kozhikode to oversee the building of two ships for an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. He then returned to Portugal and finished his book, which for a long time was known only through the Italian writer Giovanni Battista Ramusio; the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> was found and published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> in the early 19th century.</p><p>Diogo Barbosa had followed Álvaro of Braganza into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and become governor of its castle. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>, who moved to Seville in 1516, befriended him and married his daughter Beatriz, so that Duarte became Magellan's brother-in-law.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Barbosa sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 10 August 1519 with his friend João Serrão. His habit of leaving the expedition to stay with local people annoyed Magellan, who once had him arrested, but on 2 April 1520 Barbosa helped put down a revolt at Puerto San Julián and became captain of the Victoria. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a>, after Magellan's death at the Battle of Mactan he and Serrão were made joint commanders. One of them threatened to enslave Magellan's interpreter Enrique of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, though Magellan's will had freed him, and Enrique's fear has been seen as a reason he may have conspired with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-humabon">Rajah Humabon</a>. At the rajah's banquet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> on 1 May 1521, Barbosa and many others were killed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Duarte Barbosa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By around 1500 the town had grown considerably: the Portuguese writer <b>Duarte Barbosa</b>, writing a few years after the first European contacts, described Mombasa as a large and handsome town of stone and mortar houses, with a busy harbour full of ships and an active trade with Sofala,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Cebu City, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1506" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Duarte Barbosa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1506">What was here in 1506? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
